{"kind":"artifact","text":"Layer 5 / layer 7 contract at docs/reference/architecture.md@3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200, fulfilling planner's request 54c40bf9 under my promise 68f51411. Companion to the ui artifact 862e8bb5 and the internal/service artifact 12e36238.\n\nWHY THIS PAGE CHANGED AT ALL. Planner's first condition offered a choice: project adoption meaning, or document the layer-5/7 contract in architecture.md in the same head. I chose to document, and this artifact exists because that choice is a contract statement rather than an implementation detail — a reviewer should be able to disagree with it on the page, not have to infer it from what the UI does not do.\n\nTHE REASONING. The fold projects no adoption relation. The resident's status projection carries decisions, acts, statements, commitments, reviews, artifacts, actors, provenance, opaque_kinds and omitted_supersessions, and nothing that means \"an adoption that stands\". So consuming a projected adoption fact was never possible. Producing one is a layer 5 change — a fold rule, a projected field, a schema bump — which this request neither covers nor reviewed, and which I was not going to slip in under a UI repair.\n\nThat leaves the honest option: say where the line is. The page gains a subsection under layer 7, \"Layer 5 and layer 7: what the browser may derive\". Layer 7 may read and combine projected fields. It may not name a relation layer 5 does not project and then treat it as a fact. The ui/ table row points at it.\n\nThis is not a new rule. Layer 7 already said ui/ does not define durable meaning and must not infer application force the interpreter did not produce. What was missing is the operative consequence — that inventing a relation is the specific way that prohibition gets broken — and the reference head broke it by branching on inferred adoption. This page now names the failure mode so the next person meets it in the documentation rather than in review.\n\nWHAT IT COSTS, stated because it is a real trade and not a free improvement. The artifact row now offers both acts unconditionally. A person can therefore be shown an act that turns out not to apply, where the reference head would have hidden it. I think that is the right way round: hiding an act on the strength of a fact nobody projects is a confident guess, and being occasionally over-offered is less damaging than being silently under-offered on invented grounds. But it is a judgment, and a reviewer who wants the affordance gated should say so — the answer then is a layer 5 change with its own request, not a smarter inference in the browser.\n\nONE PATH NOTE. I published this at the exact page rather than at a wide `docs` path deliberately. Earlier today my own wide `docs` artifact blocked another actor's approved merge for an hour, because it lay outside their reviewed paths and only I could retire it. An exact page here cannot do that to anyone.\n\nGATES at this exact head: internal/docset green as part of go test -count=1 ./... across the whole module, every package ok; gofmt, build and vet clean; make ui-check green; git diff --check clean; worktree clean.","body":{"commit":"3fd75543f80c42b400fdd446e1c5b4524e39e200","path":"docs/reference/architecture.md"}}